{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ST8hL1fqFCWsH4Zkc1QSUL","type":"tool_result","content":"### Whether milestone clubs fit depends on the lattice pitch, not canvas size: 6, 8 or 10 clubs are refused on mid-size canvases, contradicting the caveat and outputHints [medium]\nChecked against dist built 10:39 from src saved 10:37:17. The source was being revised while I reviewed, so these runs are on the current code. node: settleLayout({milestones:'6xR25, 4xR50, 3xR100, 2xR250, 1xR500, 1xR1000, 5xV25, 3xV50'}, 1200, 900) throws 'milestones cannot be fitted on 1200x900 above the 13px readability floor: shorten it or render a larger canvas'. The same 8 clubs at 960x540 render fine. Ten clubs: 1440x900 refused, 1280x720 and 960x540 ok. Six clubs (6xR25, 4xR50, 3xR100, 2xR250, 1xR500, 5xV25): 1000x800 and 1290x1000 refused, 960x540 ok. CLI confirms: 'm0saic make ... -w 1200 -h 900 --props @eight.props.json' exits 1, '-w 1000 -h 800' with six clubs exits 1, 960x540 with eight exits 0 (logs in journal/2026-10-06/scratch/review/). Cause: layoutWeeklyRunReport(...,1200,900,{x:1,y:1}) fits with an exact 13px caption on a 120x64 badge. At the settled pitch {4,3}, snapGroup takes the step count 21 down to the 5-smooth 20, so the badge is 60px tall. The caption cap floor(60*0.26/1.22) = 12 is below small = 13, and line 627 'if (!A.fits) unfit(\"milestones\", small)' refuses. Square and tall shapes have no fallback arrangement. Expected per WHY caveats (line 751) and outputHints.note (line 770): 'Eight or more clubs are refused on a 480x270 thumbnail, ten on 640x360', so every larger canvas should render. The error's advice 'render a larger canvas' is also wrong here, since smaller canvases succeed. 30-build.md does not mention these refusals.\nFIX: Make the fit check robust to the snap. In arrange(), when the snapped badge misses `small`, try the next 5-smooth step count up that still fits inside slot+slack, or reclaim height (a smaller rowGap, or the band-title spacing) before refusing. Alternatively, size the pre-snap badge with a one-pitch margin so the snap cannot push the caption under the floor. If refusals stay, state the real rule in the caveat, outputHints.note and error message: refusals depend on canvas size and lattice, not only on the thumbnails.\n\n### counts passed as a JSON string, which json-type props may legally be, is refused, so the card cannot render from such an editor [medium]\nnode: normalizeWeeklyRunReport({counts: JSON.stringify({finishers:214,newPbs:38,firstTimers:27,visitors:19,volunteers:31,firstTimeVolunteers:4})}) throws 'counts must be an object with the keys finishers, volunteers, ...' (parseCounts, line 238). CLI: --props '{\"counts\":\"{\\\"finishers\\\":214,...}\"}' exits 1 with the same error (scratch/review/countsstr.log). The json-prop-type reference (node_modules/@m0saic/knowledge/docs/templates/reference/json-prop-type.md) says 'editors may deliver an already-parsed object *or* a raw JSON string ... Do not assume you received an object.' A host using the plain code-editor fallback for `counts` would get a refusal on every render. The repo's powerlifting-meet-recap pickAttempts already JSON.parses string values.\nFIX: At the top of parseCounts: if (typeof value === \"string\") { try { value = JSON.parse(value) } catch { fail(\"counts\", \"is not valid JSON: ...\") } }. Then run the existing object checks. Add a test that passes counts as a string.\n\n### Sanity checks miss newPbs + firstTimers > finishers, so impossible weeks reach the card [low]\nnode: normalizeWeeklyRunReport({counts:{finishers:214,newPbs:200,firstTimers:200,visitors:19,volunteers:31,firstTimeVolunteers:4}}) is accepted, and the card prints 200 new PBs and 200 first timers out of 214 finishers. On a results page a finisher is either a first timer at the event or can set a new PB there, never both, so newPbs + firstTimers <= finishers always holds. parseCounts (lines 251-254) only checks each count against finishers on its own. The header comment promises 'the sanity checks a results page always passes' and says that a silently accepted typo 'puts a wrong number on a public page'. A slipped digit (e.g. 138 for 38) still passes.\nFIX: After the per-key checks: if (out.newPbs + out.firstTimers > out.finishers) fail(\"counts.newPbs\", `${out.newPbs} new PBs plus ${out.firstTimers} first timers is more than the ${out.finishers} finishers.`). Add it to the refusal test.\n\n### snapGroup's '5-smooth sides' claim is false when the parent pitch is not 5-smooth: child canvases come out rough [low]\nsnapGroup (lines 350-366) takes the step COUNT down to a 5-smooth number but multiplies it by pitch. When settleLayout lands on pitch 7, 13, 17 or 23, the child sizes are rough. Rendered doc.children sizes from WeeklyRunReportV1.render: 828x1792 (iPhone XR story) with footer \"\" gives pitch {2,7} and tiles 360x448 and 360x315, badges 128x126. 1125x2436 with footer \"\" gives pitch {3,7} and 486x630, 486x420, 162x175. 1001x1001 with date \"\" gives pitch {7,1} and 448x225, 210x200, 140x162. 1079x1079 with date \"\" gives pitch {13,1}, and the stat gutters become 26/13/26 px while hero edges sit 12px inside the band and stat edges 1px outside it. The renders work (1001x1001 CLI exit 0, no warning). But the code comment ('each ON the parent lattice with 5-smooth sides') and 30-build.md ('every child canvas is 5-smooth') are not true there, and latticeSmooth would fail at those canvases. The test only checks the seven contract canvases.\nFIX: Choose sizes, not step counts: pick the largest w <= steps*pitch with w % pitch === 0 and w 5-smooth, e.g. iterate k downward until isSmooth(k*pitch). Or, when pitch itself is not 5-smooth, snap to lcm(pitch, a smooth unit) or fall back to exact placement on that axis. Extend the child-smoothness test to a few odd canvases (828x1792, 1125x2436).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b12b7f98-286e-4a2c-8ef9-9c2a336f1aee","uuid":"115e1804-5d45-4d33-b3eb-93a2b8ea9eb7","timestamp":"2026-10-06T20:32:26.327Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"### Whether milestone clubs fit depends on the lattice pitch, not canvas size: 6, 8 or 10 clubs are refused on mid-size canvases, contradicting the caveat and outputHints [medium]\nChecked against dist built 10:39 from src saved 10:37:17. The source was being revised while I reviewed, so these runs are on the current code. node: settleLayout({milestones:'6xR25, 4xR50, 3xR100, 2xR250, 1xR500, 1xR1000, 5xV25, 3xV50'}, 1200, 900) throws 'milestones cannot be fitted on 1200x900 above the 13px readability floor: shorten it or render a larger canvas'. The same 8 clubs at 960x540 render fine. Ten clubs: 1440x900 refused, 1280x720 and 960x540 ok. Six clubs (6xR25, 4xR50, 3xR100, 2xR250, 1xR500, 5xV25): 1000x800 and 1290x1000 refused, 960x540 ok. CLI confirms: 'm0saic make ... -w 1200 -h 900 --props @eight.props.json' exits 1, '-w 1000 -h 800' with six clubs exits 1, 960x540 with eight exits 0 (logs in journal/2026-10-06/scratch/review/). Cause: layoutWeeklyRunReport(...,1200,900,{x:1,y:1}) fits with an exact 13px caption on a 120x64 badge. At the settled pitch {4,3}, snapGroup takes the step count 21 down to the 5-smooth 20, so the badge is 60px tall. The caption cap floor(60*0.26/1.22) = 12 is below small = 13, and line 627 'if (!A.fits) unfit(\"milestones\", small)' refuses. Square and tall shapes have no fallback arrangement. Expected per WHY caveats (line 751) and outputHints.note (line 770): 'Eight or more clubs are refused on a 480x270 thumbnail, ten on 640x360', so every larger canvas should render. The error's advice 'render a larger canvas' is also wrong here, since smaller canvases succeed. 30-build.md does not mention these refusals.\nFIX: Make the fit check robust to the snap. In arrange(), when the snapped badge misses `small`, try the next 5-smooth step count up that still fits inside slot+slack, or reclaim height (a smaller rowGap, or the band-title spacing) before refusing. Alternatively, size the pre-snap badge with a one-pitch margin so the snap cannot push the caption under the floor. If refusals stay, state the real rule in the caveat, outputHints.note and error message: refusals depend on canvas size and lattice, not only on the thumbnails.\n\n### counts passed as a JSON string, which json-type props may legally be, is refused, so the card cannot render from such an editor [medium]\nnode: normalizeWeeklyRunReport({counts: JSON.stringify({finishers:214,newPbs:38,firstTimers:27,visitors:19,volunteers:31,firstTimeVolunteers:4})}) throws 'counts must be an object with the keys finishers, volunteers, ...' (parseCounts, line 238). CLI: --props '{\"counts\":\"{\\\"finishers\\\":214,...}\"}' exits 1 with the same error (scratch/review/countsstr.log). The json-prop-type reference (node_modules/@m0saic/knowledge/docs/templates/reference/json-prop-type.md) says 'editors may deliver an already-parsed object *or* a raw JSON string ... Do not assume you received an object.' A host using the plain code-editor fallback for `counts` would get a refusal on every render. The repo's powerlifting-meet-recap pickAttempts already JSON.parses string values.\nFIX: At the top of parseCounts: if (typeof value === \"string\") { try { value = JSON.parse(value) } catch { fail(\"counts\", \"is not valid JSON: ...\") } }. Then run the existing object checks. Add a test that passes counts as a string.\n\n### Sanity checks miss newPbs + firstTimers > finishers, so impossible weeks reach the card [low]\nnode: normalizeWeeklyRunReport({counts:{finishers:214,newPbs:200,firstTimers:200,visitors:19,volunteers:31,firstTimeVolunteers:4}}) is accepted, and the card prints 200 new PBs and 200 first timers out of 214 finishers. On a results page a finisher is either a first timer at the event or can set a new PB there, never both, so newPbs + firstTimers <= finishers always holds. parseCounts (lines 251-254) only checks each count against